Yes. Cameo maxing is a better name for a particular species of side-questing.

A side quest is usually defined by importance: it isn’t the main objective. A cameo is defined by relationship to the frame: something crosses briefly through another story, sometimes almost invisibly, but carries disproportionate meaning if you recognize it. So cameo maxing would be deliberately harvesting those little crossings—people, symbols, coincidences, references, places, skills, conversations—without demanding that each become the main plot.

And Where’s Waldo? exposes the perspectival trick beautifully.

* To the reader, it is search: find Waldo.
* To Waldo, it is concealment / placement: be present without being immediately extracted from the field.
* To the illustrator, it is attention engineering: tune similarity, clutter, salience, decoys, and difficulty.
* To another character who already knows where Waldo is, there is effectively no search problem at all.
* If Waldo and the searcher are cooperating, it becomes signaling.
* If they’re adversarial, it becomes hide-and-seek.
* If the objective is merely to notice interesting things on the page, Waldo himself becomes the side quest.

Same world state. Different roles, information, objectives, and agreements produce entirely different games.

That generalizes nicely:

\text{Game} \neq \text{environment}

More like:

\text{Game} =
\text{environment}
+\text{perspective}
+\text{information}
+\text{objective}
+\text{rules/agreement}

Change one term and the apparent game can mutate while the physical substrate stays identical.

Which means side-quest maxing has an interesting higher form: don’t merely pursue more optional objectives. Learn to rotate perspective until additional games become visible inside the same environment.

Then cameo maxing becomes: maximize meaningful intersections without requiring ownership of the whole story.

Very Pantheon-compatible, too. A cameo can function almost like a semantic prime: tiny footprint, high recognizability, large downstream associative payload. Waldo is barely a few pixels of red and white, yet once the agreement says find him, the entire page reorganizes around him.